Access to higher education in \acro{STEM} fields for visually impaired
students is often obstructed by institutional inertia and inaccessible
visual workflows. This paper, reflecting on our presentation and
subsequent learnings at the \TeX\ User Group's 2026 conference, outlines a
code-based approach to circumventing these barriers. By treating visual
layouts and spatial mathematics as structured data via \AllTeX,
Markdown, and structural \acro{OCR}, we propose an ecosystem where
accessibility is baked into the source code.

While a comprehensive study on these methodologies is currently being
authored for the University of Alberta's Undergraduate Research
Initiative (\acro{URI}), this article serves as an intermediate
reflection of our findings, enriched by the invaluable sounding board of
the \AllTeX\ community.
